The '''2014 UEFA Europa League final''' was the final match of the [[2013–14 UEFA Europa League]], the 43rd season of [[Europe]]'s secondary club [[association football|football]] tournament organised by [[UEFA]], and the fifth season since it was renamed from the UEFA Cup to the [[UEFA Europa League]]. It was played at the [[Juventus Stadium]] in [[Turin]], Italy on 14 May 2014,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/season=2014/accesslist/index.html |title=2013/14 UEFA Europa League access list |publisher=[[UEFA]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140407032743/http://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/season%3D2014/accesslist/index.html |archive-date=7 April 2014}}</ref> between Spanish side [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] and Portuguese side [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]]. Sevilla won the match 4–2 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]], following a 0–0 draw after [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/may/14/sevilla-benfica-europa-league-final-match-report|title=Sevilla take the penalty prize as Guttmann's 'curse' does for Benfica|date=14 May 2014|work=The Guardian|access-date=15 May 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27375727|title=Sevilla 0-0 Benfica (AET, 4-2 on pens)|date=14 May 2014|work=BBC Sport |access-date=15 May 2014}}</ref>

Sevilla secured their third title in eight years, after winning the competition in [[2006 UEFA Cup final|2006]] and [[2007 UEFA Cup final|2007]]. With this triumph, they joined [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] ([[1977 UEFA Cup final|1977]], [[1990 UEFA Cup final|1990]], [[1993 UEFA Cup final|1993]]), [[Inter Milan]] ([[1991 UEFA Cup final|1991]], [[1994 UEFA Cup final|1994]], [[1998 UEFA Cup final|1998]]) and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] ([[1973 UEFA Cup final|1973]], [[1976 UEFA Cup final|1976]], [[2001 UEFA Cup final|2001]]) as the teams with the most wins. Benfica lost their second consecutive UEFA Europa League final, following their defeat against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in the [[2013 UEFA Europa League final|2013 final]]. Including their runner-up finish in [[1983 UEFA Cup final|1983]], Benfica is the team with the most lost finals in the competition.

As the winners, Sevilla earned the right to play against [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League]] winners [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] in the [[2014 UEFA Super Cup]].

==Venue==
[[File:2021–22 UEFA Women's Champions League Final in Turin, Italy 1.jpg|thumb|270x270px|The Juventus Stadium in Turin, Italy, hosted the final.]]
The [[Juventus Stadium]] in [[Turin]], Italy, was chosen as the venue of the match at a UEFA Executive Committee meeting in [[Istanbul]], Turkey, on 20 March 2012.<ref>{{cite web |title=UEFA Executive Committee agenda for Istanbul meeting |url=http://www.uefa.org/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=1766100.html |publisher=UEFA.org (Union of European Football Associations) |date=9 March 2012 |access-date=18 May 2014 |archive-date=24 April 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150424145849/http://www.uefa.org/mediaservices/mediareleases/newsid=1766100.html |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Turin to stage 2014 UEFA Europa League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/01fb-0e7d592f37d2-fab4de0bb9bb-1000--turin-to-stage-2014-uefa-europa-league-final/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=20 March 2012 }}</ref> It is the home stadium of [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] since 2011. This was the first time that a one-legged final was hosted in Turin. Previous UEFA Cup finals contested over two legs have had one of their matches played in Turin. The first legs of the [[1977 UEFA Cup final]] and the [[1990 UEFA Cup final]], both contested by Juventus, were played at the Stadio Comunale (now the [[Stadio Olimpico di Torino]]). The first leg of the [[1992 UEFA Cup final]], contested by [[Torino FC|Torino]], and the second leg of the [[1993 UEFA Cup final]], contested by Juventus, were played at the [[Stadio delle Alpi]], which has been demolished to make way for the Juventus Stadium.<ref>{{cite web |title=Previous European finals in Turin |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/025a-0ea7b98f938d-150124fadc7e-1000--previous-european-finals-in-turin/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=1 May 2014 }}</ref>

==Background==
After a comeback by their opponents [[Valencia CF|Valencia]], who had lost the first leg 2–0, [[Sevilla FC|Sevilla]] secured their presence in the final after [[Stéphane Mbia]]'s injury-time header qualified them on [[Away goals rule|away goals]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27226541|title=Valencia 3 Sevilla 1|publisher=BBC Sport|date=1 May 2014}}</ref> Sevilla had previously played in two UEFA Cup finals, winning both times in [[2006 UEFA Cup final|2006]] and [[2007 UEFA Cup final|2007]],<ref name="benfica-sevilla">{{cite web |title=Benfica and Sevilla to meet in Turin final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0215-0e8a8c9e80fc-fe7d22ca140a-1000--benfica-and-sevilla-to-meet-in-turin-final/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=1 May 2014 }}</ref> and were aiming to become the fourth team to [[UEFA Cup and Europa League records and statistics|win three UEFA Cup/Europa League titles]], after [[Juventus FC|Juventus]], [[Inter Milan]] and [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Hat-trick trio: The three-time winners |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c51058057d2-f2453ea8b6f7-1000--sevilla-make-it-four-three-time-winners/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=7 May 2014 }}</ref>

[[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] reached their second consecutive Europa League final,<ref name="stats">{{cite web|title=Semi-finals: second-leg stats and facts|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0215-0e8a9176e83f-c8f5a8749350-1000--semi-finals-second-leg-stats-and-facts/|publisher=UEFA|access-date=3 May 2014|date=2 May 2014}}</ref> after defeating [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] 2–1 on aggregate and denying their opponents a chance to play the final at their home stadium.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/27226534|title=Juventus 0 Benfica 0|publisher=BBC Sport|date=1 May 2014}}</ref> It was the first time a club has reached consecutive finals in the competition, having featured in the Champions League group stage on each occasion. Both of their previous UEFA Cup/Europa League finals, in [[1983 UEFA Cup final|1983]] and [[2013 UEFA Europa League final|2013]], ended in defeats. They had also played in seven [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] finals ([[1961 European Cup final|1961]], [[1962 European Cup final|1962]], [[1963 European Cup final|1963]], [[1965 European Cup final|1965]], [[1968 European Cup final|1968]], [[1988 European Cup final|1988]], [[1990 European Cup final|1990]]). After winning successive European titles in 1961 and 1962, they had lost seven straight major European finals.<ref name="benfica-sevilla"/>

The final was Sevilla's 19th match in the competition, having started their participation in the third qualifying round against Montenegrin side [[OFK Titograd|Mladost Podgorica]].<ref name="stats"/> They only qualified for the competition after [[Málaga CF|Málaga]] were banned and [[Rayo Vallecano]] were denied a UEFA licence.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://beta.thescore.com/news/493246|title=Sevilla and Benfica to meet in Europa League final|publisher=theScore.com|date=2 May 2014}}</ref> Benfica transitioned from the [[2013–14 UEFA Champions League group stage|Champions League group stage]], after finishing third in their group, behind [[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]] and [[Olympiacos F.C.|Olympiacos]]. They became the first team to reach the Europa League final without conceding a defeat, registering six wins and two draws in eight knockout phase matches.<ref name="stats"/>

The only previous meeting between Sevilla and Benfica in European competition was in the [[1957–58 European Cup]] preliminary round. The first leg at [[Estadio de Nervión]], won by Sevilla 3–1, marked the European debut of both clubs. The second leg at [[Estádio da Luz (1954)|Estádio da Luz]] ended 0–0, giving Sevilla the victory on aggregate, and they later reached the quarter-finals before losing to eventual champions [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Benfica and Sevilla going back to the start |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0215-0e8a9db2ce29-6fa0e222bb75-1000--benfica-and-sevilla-going-back-to-the-start/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=5 May 2014 }}</ref>

