==Pre-match==

===Ambassador===
[[File:Ciro Ferrara.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Ciro Ferrara]] was the ambassador for the final.]]

Former Italy international and Juventus player [[Ciro Ferrara]], who won the UEFA Cup in 1989 with [[SSC Napoli|Napoli]], was named as the ambassador for the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Ferrara picked as Turin final ambassador |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/020c-0e14503e4823-fc1aa57048f8-1000--ferrara-picked-as-turin-final-ambassador/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=30 August 2013 }}</ref>

===Identity===
UEFA unveiled the visual identity of the final on 30 August 2013, the same day as the group stage draw.<ref>{{cite web |title=Visual identity for Turin final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/020c-0e14506e4a6f-3098490c7286-1000--visual-identity-for-turin-final/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=30 August 2013 }}</ref>

===Ticketing===
The international ticket sales phase for the general public ran from 27 February to 25 March 2014. Tickets were available in four price categories: [[Euro|€]]150, €100, €70, and €45.<ref>{{cite web |title=Turin final tickets go on international sale |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0212-0e89143f34b5-aa57ab203b78-1000--turin-final-tickets-go-on-international-sale/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=27 February 2014 }}</ref>

===Officials===
German referee [[Felix Brych]] was named by UEFA on 7 May 2014 as the referee of the final.<ref name="referees">{{cite web |title=Brych to referee UEFA Europa League final |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0215-0e15866d0a91-22c4d1f1c33f-1000--brych-to-referee-uefa-europa-league-final/ |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=7 May 2014 }}</ref> The rest of the refereeing team are fellow countrymen Mark Borsch and Stefan Lupp as assistant referees, Tobias Welz and Bastian Dankert as additional assistant referees, Thorsten Schiffner as reserve assistant referee, and Serbia's Milorad Mažić as the fourth official.

===Team selection===
Benfica were not able to play either [[Enzo Pérez]] or [[Lazar Marković]], both of whom were sent off in the second leg of their semi-final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Match Press Kit |url=https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2014/E/E_2012680_PK.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200306234610/https://www.uefa.com/printoutfiles/competitions/uefaeuropaleague/2014/E/E_2012680_PK.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 March 2020 |publisher=UEFA.com (Union of European Football Associations) |date=14 May 2014 |access-date=14 May 2014 }}</ref> [[Eduardo Salvio]], who was booked in that match, was also suspended.<ref>{{cite news |title=Juventus 0-0 Benfica |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27226534 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=1 May 2014 |access-date=2 May 2014 }}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary ===
At full-time, the game was locked at 0–0. After a further 30 minutes of extra time, both sides were still scoreless.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/europa-league/10830506/Benfica-vs-Sevilla-Europa-League-final-live.html|title=Sevilla v Benfica, Europa League final 2014: as it happened|newspaper=Telegraph.co.uk|access-date=2016-11-02}}</ref> This meant the match was the first final to end goalless and the first to be decided by penalty shoot outs.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/news/0250-0c5106f8ab42-2858ce4b1926-1000--uefa-europa-league-final-stats-and-facts/|title=UEFA Europa League - News – UEFA.com|last=uefa.com|date=2014-05-15|newspaper=[[UEFA]]|access-date=2016-11-02}}</ref> Sevilla won the penalty shoot out 4–2, their goals coming from [[Carlos Bacca]], [[Stéphane Mbia]], [[Coke (footballer)|Coke]] and [[Kevin Gameiro]]. [[Lima (footballer, born 1983)|Lima]] and [[Luisão]] scored for Benfica, while Sevilla goalkeeper [[Beto (footballer, born 1982)|Beto]] saved goals from [[Óscar Cardozo]] and [[Rodrigo (footballer, born 1991)|Rodrigo]].<ref name=":0" /> [[Paul Gardner (journalist)|Paul Gardner]] writing for [[Soccer America]] opined that the [[Assistant referee (association football)|assistant referee]] standing on the goal line allowed Beto to advance too far when he saved the two goals and that Benfica should have been allowed to take the shots again.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.socceramerica.com/article/58069/brazen-goalkeeper-cheating-helps-sevilla-win-europ.html|title=Brazen goalkeeper cheating helps Sevilla win Europa League|last=Gardner|first=Paul|date=15 May 2014|publisher=SoccerAmerica|access-date=17 May 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.vavel.com/br/futebol-internacional/350544-com-arbitragem-polemica-e-muito-drama-sevilla-bate-benfica-nos-penaltis-e-conquista-a-uel.html|title=Com arbitragem polêmica e muito drama, Sevilla bate Benfica nos pênaltis e conquista a UEL|date=14 May 2014|website=vavel.com|publisher=VAVEL Brasil|language=pt|trans-title=With a polemic officiating and much drama, Sevilla beats Benfica on penalties and wins the UEL|access-date=23 January 2015}}</ref>

